WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:08.330
So we've talked a little bit about the kind of simple code that you would use to get some of this stuff working but it's worth actually looking at the editor itself.

2
00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:15.060
The easiest way to make a widget is through the actual Blueprint content flow.

3
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:21.059
You can again make classes that inherit from these things in C++ but just as a kind of quicker way to demonstrate it.

4
00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:28.059
If we go to... I'm just going to go into the 1.1.0.5 folder because you know that's at least vaguely the right area.

5
00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:32.060
But you would go to User Interface here by right-clicking and go to Widget Blueprint.

6
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:37.060
And then you're presented with a class selector very much like when you're making an actor class.

7
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.060
Or, well, just a new Blueprint, sorry.

8
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.060
But you'll see that the hierarchy is slightly different.

9
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.060
Again, we've got our visual as our root.

10
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.060
And then we have Widget.

11
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.060
And again, you'll note that neither of these two things are viable options to select from.

12
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:58.060
It's only at the point that we select User Widget that we can actually create an instance of this class.

13
00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:02.060
And if we were then to select that, we would then get that new widget created for us.

14
00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:07.060
So if I just called this BP underscore My New Widget.

15
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:14.060
Although I say BP, actually the typical naming convention would either be WBP for Widget Blueprint.

16
00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:20.060
Or sometimes if you want to be a little bit more shorthand, W underscore Widget.

17
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:27.060
In my own work, I've typically used double W to say that this is a Blueprint-only widget.

18
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.060
And WPP to indicate that this is a Widget Blueprint that inherits from C++.

19
00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:36.060
From a C++ widget that isn't just User Widget, but is my own C++ class.

20
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.060
But that is purely my own workflow kind of thing.

21
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.060
So that's not necessarily something you need to adopt yourselves.

22
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:49.019
So just talking through the window that we'll see when we first open our widget up.

23
00:01:50.019 --> 00:01:54.019
I will quickly do this just to show you that we are talking about the same screen here.

24
00:01:54.019 --> 00:01:58.019
But it's kind of easier to go with the presentation where we've got this kind of markup on all of these sections.

25
00:01:58.019 --> 00:02:03.019
So right at the top of the section we've got our kind of standard toolbar.

26
00:02:03.019 --> 00:02:08.020
This is pretty much as we would expect to find really across all the engine.

27
00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:12.020
And most pieces suffer to be honest, like our file edit section.

28
00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:16.020
Some of these things do apply are kind of semi-contextual.

29
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:21.020
So they may have options that aren't necessarily available outside of UMG.

30
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.020
So just kind of worth mentioning that.

31
00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:28.370
Underneath we've got the point where they start to get a little bit more specific to UMG.

32
00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:32.370
So we've got the toolbar. This contains a kind of number of common functions.

33
00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:38.370
We've obviously got our save, our search, compiling our Blueprint, diff.

34
00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:42.370
So this is obviously only going to be relevant when we're connected to source control.

35
00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.370
But you can diff both Blueprints and user widgets.

36
00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.370
It's actually got quite developed functionality.

37
00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:52.370
This didn't always used to be as good as it is now.

38
00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:55.370
But you can diff widgets quite successfully with each other.

39
00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.370
They're particularly good at diffing the code itself, the Blueprint code.

40
00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:04.370
Changes to the actual widget hierarchy are represented.

41
00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.370
And you can see differences in the values and any widgets you might have added.

42
00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.370
The visualization of them isn't quite so developed.

43
00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.370
So it's not perfect, but it will get you a long way there.

44
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.370
And the information is available to you there.

45
00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.370
So that's a lot more helpful than it not being there.

46
00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.370
Then we've got our standard play in editor.

47
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.370
Next we have our dropdown.

48
00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:30.370
So this would be familiar to anyone who's spent this time working with debugging actors.

49
00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:32.370
But this is just to select an instance.

50
00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.370
So if, for example, you're debugging a widget and there are two copies of that widget,

51
00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.370
because you're creating two health bars, one for each player,

52
00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:44.370
you could select which of the two health bars you're trying to debug at the moment.

53
00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:51.560
Then we have our widget reflector, which is a button that is unique to UMG.

54
00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.560
And it's worth just mentioning this because it's a very, very powerful tool.

55
00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.560
I'll talk a little bit more about this, but it is worth checking this out.

56
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.560
This is essentially a debugging tool that lets you kind of debug what you're seeing at runtime.

57
00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:08.560
I'll come back to this in a little bit, but just worth at least mentioning that there is a button to open up your widget reflector.

58
00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:14.560
It can also be done through a console command, which is, again, we'll circle back to this towards the end of the presentation.

59
00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:22.560
But if this is actually the example of my networking course, but it's kind of not too important.

60
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:30.560
If I just go to widget reflector and type that in a console command, you can also open the widget reflector this way.

61
00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.560
It's open on my other screen, but you just have to trust me that opened.

62
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.560
So, yeah, we'll circle back to that.

63
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.560
So, yes, that's kind of second section in the top right.

64
00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:49.560
We've got the switcher that lets us switch between our designer, which is the kind of more UMG.

65
00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:56.560
Yeah, the more UMG kind of unique view where we're kind of doing all the visual editing and our graph view,

66
00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.560
which is going to look a lot more like a standard typical blueprint graph view.

67
00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:07.560
So if you spent any time editing blueprints, that's graph is going to look a lot more like the other blueprints you would have worked in before.

68
00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:12.100
And yeah, so it's going to have the same things exposed.

69
00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:18.100
There are functions, variables and really access to any of the kind of C++ behaviour,

70
00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:28.100
whether that's, you know, you would also be exposing any kind of C++ functionality or variables for any class we might have inherited from there.

71
00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.870
Then on the back over to the left here, we've got our fourth section.

72
00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:35.870
This is the palette. This is essentially, well, as the name kind of implies,

73
00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:42.870
it's a palette of pre-made widgets that we can use to define the kind of structure

74
00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:46.870
that we can build up a more complex picture for what our widget actually looks like.

75
00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:50.870
Really, this will show anything that inherits from UWidget.

76
00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:55.870
Some of the kind of common things is containers such as vertical and horizontal boxes,

77
00:05:55.870 --> 00:06:00.870
scroll boxes, overlays, borders, spaces. We've kind of already talked about a lot of these.

78
00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:05.870
It'll also contain any user-generated widgets at the point that you make your own user blueprints.

79
00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:10.870
They will come out in their own section here.

80
00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:16.870
And yeah, I kind of think that's everything really to say about palette. Below the palette, we've also got the hierarchy.

81
00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:21.870
So where palette is the kind of options available to us for the widgets that we can drag in,

82
00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.870
our hierarchy actually represents our current view of our widget as it is designed.

83
00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:32.870
So this is the kind of like the tree that defines how our widget is actually laid out.

84
00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:38.870
So kind of getting into the detail of the hierarchy is maybe something better done in live.

85
00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:43.870
But this would look a little bit similar to maybe if you've worked in DCC packages before

86
00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.870
or in your blueprint editor when you're adding scene components.

87
00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:51.870
This is in a hierarchy, so widgets can be inside of other widgets.

88
00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:55.870
It depends on the kind of widget type, whether or not a widget can have no widgets within it,

89
00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:02.870
whether it can have several or just one. But typically a kind of more complicated hierarchy would have several elements in it.

90
00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:06.870
In fact, if I just quickly jump over to a pre-existing example.

91
00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:14.870
So if I go to our health bar widget and I go to a segmented bar widget.

92
00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:20.870
Oh, that's too simple. Do I have any? Oh, I definitely do.

93
00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:33.560
If I go to focus navigation, this is what a kind of slightly more complicated hierarchy might actually look like.

94
00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:36.560
Where you have, again, hierarchy being the key here.

95
00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:39.560
We've got these dropdowns for where widgets are socketed inside of other widgets.

96
00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:42.560
So we have a horizontal box containing several elements.

97
00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.560
We have a vertical box containing several elements.

98
00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:51.560
You can even have, you know, a vertical box containing a horizontal box containing a scroll box.

99
00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:56.560
So, you know, we're not limited to kind of one level of depth here in one.

100
00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.560
And you can kind of infer that from the fact that we have everything inside a canvas panel.

101
00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.620
So we've already got at least three layers of depth here.

102
00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:08.620
So this is, again, just the kind of editor time representation of how this widget might look.

103
00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:14.620
Obviously, there's still the capacity here to add new widgets at runtime.

104
00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:19.620
So this is only the kind of more static version of this widget.

105
00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:25.620
If you're adding things at runtime, then that's obviously not going to be able to be reflected here.

106
00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:31.680
So I think that's our hierarchy.

107
00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.679
Then, yes, section six is basically just our visual designer.

108
00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:37.679
So this is a visual representation of everything that's in our hierarchy.

109
00:08:37.679 --> 00:08:43.679
Again, just to quickly jump over, we can see for every widget that is represented in our hierarchy,

110
00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:48.679
that is selectable and visualizable here.

111
00:08:48.679 --> 00:08:50.679
We have various settings to control this space.

112
00:08:50.679 --> 00:08:53.679
So this is a little bit like a canvas for us to drag things into.

113
00:08:53.679 --> 00:08:57.679
You can define things like the kind of size of this area by default.

114
00:08:57.679 --> 00:09:01.679
But between the two of these, this is a little bit like a world eyeliner,

115
00:09:01.679 --> 00:09:07.679
whereas this is a kind of game viewpoint if you want to turn it into a level of context.

116
00:09:07.679 --> 00:09:13.679
And working between the two can be quite a potent way to navigate between things that either might be hidden

117
00:09:13.679 --> 00:09:17.679
or just a little bit easy to jump back and forth between finding things

118
00:09:17.679 --> 00:09:24.809
and then clicking on the bits that are visually relevant.

119
00:09:24.809 --> 00:09:32.809
Yeah, and then last of all, we've got the details panel, which will be familiar to anyone who's spent any time working in the world outliner or the level editor.

120
00:09:32.809 --> 00:09:33.809
And it does the kind of same job here.

121
00:09:33.809 --> 00:09:40.809
So for any item that you currently have selected, the details panel will display all of the variable,

122
00:09:40.809 --> 00:09:48.809
all the bits of information, all the variables about that particular widget in a kind of editable way.

123
00:09:48.809 --> 00:09:57.809
Typically for widgets, this might be things like colour pickers, this might be various values that you've set, bits of information relevant to the slots.

124
00:09:57.809 --> 00:10:04.870
We've already talked about this idea of, sorry, jumping ahead a slide, we've already talked about this idea of slots for panels,

125
00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:13.870
but it's just worth quickly touching on that, actually, that when something that is kind of a little bit more different about UMG that you might not necessarily get used to

126
00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:20.870
is that when you're looking at any given widget, usually one of the first things you'll see is the slot.

127
00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:25.870
This is not information that is specific to this type of widget.

128
00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:30.870
This slot information is made available to it based on the widget that is inside of.

129
00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:39.870
So this exact same, this is a, well, if I go to like a text widget here, for example, you can see that we've got the slot, which is a horizontal box slot.

130
00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:45.870
If I drag this text widget inside the canvas panel, now it doesn't have a horizontal box slot anymore, it has a canvas panel slot.

131
00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:51.870
So whereas the rest of the details panel is kind of talking about this widget itself,

132
00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:58.870
if this widget is inside a panel, which the vast majority of widgets are, the vast majority of the time,

133
00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:05.870
the kind of first section tends to be describing the slot, which is information about the thing it is inside of

134
00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:09.870
and what capabilities it has to kind of control the way it's laid out within that thing.

135
00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:15.870
So within the context of a horizontal box, you can say, you know, is this thing trying to demand its own space?

136
00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:18.870
Is it trying to fill up all the space available?

137
00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:23.870
How much padding does it have from nearby content from the kind of slot that's been given to it?

138
00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:28.870
Should it try and lay itself out like centered? Should it try and fill the entire space?

139
00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:32.870
Should it lay itself out vertically centered, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera?

140
00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:40.870
It very much depends contextually on what kind of panel it's inside of for what information is available to it through the slot.

141
00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:45.740
And then last of all, we have the animation window.

142
00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:48.740
So this won't necessarily appear straight away.

143
00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:55.740
I think typically when you open the UMG editor, it's not actually going to be there, but it's just down here in this little dropdown.

144
00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:57.740
So this is kind of an expandable window.

145
00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:59.740
This is a little bit like the content drawer.

146
00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:02.740
If you press control space, it will just temporarily open up.

147
00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:05.740
I think control shift space opens up the animation window.

148
00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:10.740
But if you are doing a lot of animation authoring that can come and become a little bit irritating.

149
00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:22.409
There is also this docking layout, which will actually turn into a kind of like a sticky window that will actually just continue perpetually sit in your editor unless you then just choose to close that window.

150
00:12:22.409 --> 00:12:32.409
So this is where you would author any animations where you would key various frames and just do all your animation kind of layout functionality.

151
00:12:32.409 --> 00:12:37.409
We'll touch a little bit more on animations later and we'll circle back to that.

152
00:12:37.409 --> 00:12:43.340
So if we were to just quickly go through a demonstration of how one might actually use some of this stuff.

153
00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:50.340
We've got some GIFs here in the presentation, but I figure I might as well kind of just do this live because it's a little bit easier.

154
00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:54.340
So we can see that it's very easy to kind of put content into a new widget.

155
00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:59.039
If I would just go back to that new widget we've made.

156
00:12:59.039 --> 00:13:03.039
Like I said, we've got our palette here. We've got a couple of different options.

157
00:13:03.039 --> 00:13:12.039
Typically you would put in some kind of root panel because normally the first thing you want in your widget isn't a widget that can't have anything else attached to it.

158
00:13:12.039 --> 00:13:14.039
It's something that can have something attached to it.

159
00:13:14.039 --> 00:13:20.039
So the kind of standard go-to is a canvas panel because canvas panels give you a lot of control.

160
00:13:20.039 --> 00:13:25.039
It's just worth mentioning that canvas panels are about the least efficient widget to use.

161
00:13:25.039 --> 00:13:30.039
So one should be a little bit cautious about using them if you don't need to.

162
00:13:30.039 --> 00:13:34.039
But just for the purposes of demonstration, it's quite an easy one to use.

163
00:13:34.039 --> 00:13:38.039
Canvas panels are essentially a panel that can have as many children as they like in it.

164
00:13:38.039 --> 00:13:42.039
But they also give that child a lot of control over how it's laid out within that widget.

165
00:13:42.039 --> 00:13:51.100
So if I was to take an image and you can see I can either drag items from my palette into my hierarchy like this.

166
00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:59.100
Or I can drag them out into my visual editor like this and I'm kind of positioning it and adding it at the same time.

167
00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:02.299
A lot of the kind of standard shortcuts here work as well.

168
00:14:02.299 --> 00:14:05.299
So if I press control D, that's going to duplicate it.

169
00:14:05.299 --> 00:14:07.299
I think by default it does tend to snap it to the top left.

170
00:14:07.299 --> 00:14:11.299
So that's just a little kind of like eccentricity that's worth bearing in mind.

171
00:14:11.299 --> 00:14:21.299
And then as you can see, by editing this, I'm affecting its slot because canvas slots have a pixel coordinate from the top left of the screen.

172
00:14:21.299 --> 00:14:32.299
But you can also, I say the top left is also kind of like an anchor that says, you know, where, how do its coordinates describe itself in relation to a certain section of the screen?

173
00:14:32.299 --> 00:14:37.299
So you can use this in a way to say that, you know, this widget should always be in the centre of the screen exactly.

174
00:14:37.299 --> 00:14:41.299
You could set its anchor to the centre and have it at 0, 0.

175
00:14:41.299 --> 00:14:44.299
And there's a couple of additional things to bear in mind, like alignment.

176
00:14:44.299 --> 00:14:49.299
So if I said that this is aligned 0.5, 0.5, that would be truly in the centre.

177
00:14:49.299 --> 00:14:51.299
So, yeah, worth experimenting.

178
00:14:51.299 --> 00:14:58.460
And a lot of these settings are going to be dependent based on what kind of panel you're working within.

179
00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:00.460
But canvas is the one that gives you the most control.

180
00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:04.460
You can easily resize things and it gives you a lot of flexibility and control.

181
00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:16.840
But again, canvases are probably the most expensive widget to use, so they should be used with a certain degree of caution.

182
00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:25.840
And then, yeah, we already talked about the fact that when we search over into our graph view, we have something that looks a lot more like our standard Blueprint code.

183
00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:27.840
We have a couple of nodes available to us.

184
00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:31.840
This is like, we will talk a little bit more about what these actually do a little bit later.

185
00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:39.840
But, you know, where we typically see our begin play and tick when you're in UMG, this would actually be construct and tick.

186
00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:43.840
But we'll talk about those in a little bit more detail in a little bit.
